AI Fake News Detection Platform — Teacher Presentation Script (English-only)

How to use this:
- Treat each “Slide” as a PPT slide.
- Read the “Say” section out loud.
- Do the “Show/Demo” steps live if you have time.
- Keep the pace: ~45–60 seconds per slide for a 8–10 minute talk.

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 1 — Title + Goal
------------------------------------------------------------
Title: AI-based Fake News Detection Platform (BERT + Web + Chrome Extension)

Say:
- “This project detects whether a news article is Fake or Real using a fine-tuned BERT model.”
- “It supports two inputs: plain text and article URL.”
- “It returns: prediction, confidence, credibility score (0–100), and an explanation.”

Show/Demo:
- Open the Chrome extension popup and point to “Analyze this page”.

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 2 — What problem are we solving?
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “Fake news spreads quickly; we want an automated first-pass filter.”
- “The goal is not perfect truth verification; it’s classification + highlighting suspicious cues.”
- “We use NLP (BERT) because it understands context better than simple keyword rules.”

Key points:
- English-only scope (bert-base-uncased).
- Output is probabilistic (confidence), not absolute truth.

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 3 — High-level architecture (end-to-end)
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “There are 5 layers: dataset → training → saved model → Flask API → UI (web + Chrome extension).”
- “Once trained, we don’t retrain every time; we load the saved model for inference.”

Show (files/folders):
- data/ (dataset pipeline)
- model/ (train + predict)
- api/ (Flask endpoints)
- frontend/ (simple web UI)
- extension/ (Chrome extension)

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 4 — Dataset used (Kaggle CSV)
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “Dataset is CSV with columns: text and label.”
- “Labels are mapped: REAL → 0, FAKE → 1.”
- “We also handle dataset quality: null rows, empty text, and class distribution.”

Mention:
- “My dataset had ~3.7k rows; 8 empty rows were dropped during cleaning.”
- “Class distribution was almost balanced.”

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 5 — Data cleaning + quality handling
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “Before training, we standardize and clean the text to reduce noise.”
- “Steps: remove URLs, remove special characters, lowercase, compress spaces.”
- “Then we drop null/empty rows, and verify labels are valid.”

Why this matters:
- “Cleaner input improves generalization and reduces overfitting to weird artifacts.”

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 6 — Train/Validation split (80/20)
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “We split data: 80% training, 20% validation.”
- “We use stratified split when possible so both sets keep the same label ratio.”
- “Validation metrics tell us how well the model generalizes to unseen samples.”

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 7 — Core AI model: BERT fine-tuning
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “We use pretrained bert-base-uncased from HuggingFace.”
- “We add a classification head for 2 classes: Real vs Fake.”
- “Fine-tuning updates BERT weights on our dataset for this specific task.”

Why BERT:
- “It captures context and relationships between words, not just keywords.”

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 8 — Training settings + metrics
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “We train for 2–4 epochs; in my final run I used 2 epochs for speed.”
- “We measure: Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score.”
- “F1 is important because it balances precision and recall.”

Mention actual results (from training log):
- Accuracy ≈ 0.9987
- Precision ≈ 0.9973
- Recall = 1.0
- F1 ≈ 0.9987

Important honesty note:
- “These are very high; it may indicate the dataset is ‘easy’ or has stylistic patterns.”
- “For real-world robustness, we can improve by merging multiple datasets/domains.”

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 9 — Model saving (so it works after restart)
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “After training, we save the model and tokenizer to disk.”
- “Key files: model.safetensors, config.json, tokenizer.json, vocab.txt.”
- “After reboot, we just load these files—no retraining needed.”

Show:
- model/artifacts/fakenews_bert/ contains the saved model.

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 10 — Prediction output: confidence + credibility + explanation
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “The model outputs probabilities using softmax.”
- “Confidence = probability for the predicted class (we display it as REAL probability in UI).”
- “Credibility score = confidence × 100, then penalties for clickbait/suspicious cues.”
- “Explanation highlights suspicious words like: shocking, breaking, exclusive.”

Note:
- “Explanation is heuristic + model probability, designed to be human-readable.”

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 11 — URL input: web scraping
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “For a URL, we fetch the page using requests and extract paragraph text from <p> tags.”
- “We limit text length to keep inference fast.”
- “Then we send the extracted text to the same predictor.”

Mention limitation:
- “Some websites block scraping or load content with JavaScript; those may fail.”

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 12 — Flask API (backend service)
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “We expose two endpoints:”
- “POST /predict: accepts raw text”
- “POST /predict_url: accepts a URL”
- “API returns JSON with prediction, confidence, credibility_score, explanation.”

Show sample response format:
{
  "prediction": "Fake/Real",
  "confidence": 0.xx,
  "credibility_score": xx,
  "explanation": "...",
  "suspicious_words": [...]
}

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 13 — Frontend + Chrome extension (user experience)
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “We have a simple web UI for manual testing.”
- “The Chrome extension is the practical part: analyze the current page in one click.”
- “It also highlights suspicious words directly on the webpage.”

Demo (best):
- Start API
- Open a news page
- Click extension → Analyze this page
- Show Real/Fake + highlight + credibility.

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 14 — How to run it tomorrow (demo checklist)
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “Step 1: Start API”
  python -m api.app --model_dir model/artifacts/fakenews_bert
- “Step 2: Open Chrome extension and analyze a page.”
- “If API isn’t running, extension won’t show results.”

Backup plan:
- Keep one sample article text ready to paste into the web UI.

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 15 — Improvements / future work (dataset + model)
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “Merge multiple datasets (different sources, topics) to generalize better.”
- “Use better explainability: sentence-level highlighting, attention visualization, or LIME/SHAP.”
- “Add domain credibility signals: source reputation, cross-checking claims, citation density.”
- “Add caching so repeated URLs are faster.”

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 16 — Limitations (important to mention)
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “Model learns patterns from training data; it can be biased if dataset is biased.”
- “It can misclassify satire, opinion pieces, or very short articles.”
- “‘Confidence’ is not absolute truth—just model probability.”
- “Scraping can fail on some sites.”

------------------------------------------------------------
Slide 17 — Final close
------------------------------------------------------------
Say:
- “This project shows a complete ML product pipeline: data prep → fine-tuning → deployment → UI/extension.”
- “The key achievement is not only training a model, but integrating it into a usable tool.”

------------------------------------------------------------
Quick Q&A prep (common teacher questions)
------------------------------------------------------------
Q: Why BERT instead of TF-IDF + SVM?
A: “BERT captures context and semantics better; TF-IDF is bag-of-words and misses meaning.”

Q: What does the credibility score mean?
A: “It’s confidence scaled to 0–100 plus penalties for clickbait cues; it’s a heuristic readability score.”

Q: Why are metrics so high?
A: “Possible dataset artifacts or easy separation. For real-world reliability we’d add more diverse datasets.”

Q: Does it verify facts?
A: “No, it predicts based on linguistic patterns; fact-checking would require external evidence and retrieval.”

